<BaseLayout title="Holding in Front — Position 3" sidebar>
  <CourseSidebar slot="sidebar" active="holding-front" />

  <h1>Holding in Front <span class="tag tag-intermediate">Intermediate · Position 3</span></h1>

  <p>Holding in Front transitions the hands from a round embrace to extending forward at shoulder height with palms facing down, as if pressing down on balloons. Imagine a balloon floating on the water's surface beneath each hand, and you gently press down to keep it from escaping. This posture begins to guide energy toward the front of the body.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📖 Book Reference</h4>
    <p>《The Way of Energy》Part Two / Part Three transitional stage</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <h4>📺 BBC Reference</h4>
    <p>《Stand Still Be Fit》Days 5-6: Holding in Front instruction and deepening</p>
  </div>

  ---

  <h2>Practice Guide</h2>

  <h3>Posture Points</h3>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Extend both hands forward at approximately <strong>shoulder height</strong> (be careful not to go too high)</li>
    <li>Palms facing down, as if pressing down on balloons floating on the water's surface</li>
    <li>Imagine the armpit balloons still supporting the arms</li>
    <li>Imagine the balloon chair under the pelvis — squat a little lower if your stamina allows, otherwise stay naturally slightly bent</li>
    <li>Head suspended, shoulders relaxed, no puffing the belly or sticking out the buttocks</li>
    <li>Weight centered, neither forward nor back — imbalance causes back pain</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Principle: Keep Smiling</h3>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>In Day 5, Master Lam offers an important suggestion: <strong>while standing, recall something happy and let a smile form on your lips and in your heart</strong>. This helps both body and mind enter the state of sung (relaxation) more naturally.</p>
  </div>

  ---

  <h2>BBC Video: Day 5</h2>
  <p>The fifth episode introduces Holding in Front. The new warm-up "Separating Hands" uses asymmetrical stretching to relax the sides of the body. Philosophy theme: returning to the root.</p>
  <VideoEmbed videoId="Fec9OIRRJPk" title="Stand Still Be Fit - Day 5" />

